TY  - JOUR
TI  - Current Diagnosis &amp; Treatment in Infectious Diseases.
AU  - Jonas F. Ludvigsson
JO  - Journal of Pediatric Gastroenterology and Nutrition
PY  - 2003
VL  - 36
IS  - 2
SP  - 298
EP  - 299
DO  - 10.1097/00005176-200302000-00031
AB  - Current Diagnosis & Treatment in Infectious Diseases. Wilson WR and Sande MA; New York: Lange/McGraw-Hill Professional Publishing; 2001:1–985. For the past 3 months I have been working in the Department of Infectious Diseases at the Orebro University Hospital to increase my knowledge of diagnostics, management, and treatment of infectious diseases. During this time, I have had the opportunity to read and consult Current Diagnosis & Treatment in Infectious Diseases (1), and I would like to share some of my impressions. I have reviewed the international edition of the book. However, with the exception of 2 or 3 chapters, US–Canadian residents wrote the 91 chapters. For that reason, the principles of diagnostics and management will mirror North American conditions and traditions. It is therefore not surprising that information on body temperature (when relevant) is given only in Fahrenheit and not Celsius degrees. The first chapters cover microbial virulence, antimicrobial therapy, infection prevention in a healthcare setting, and laboratory diagnosis. The chapter discussing antibiotics is short; I would have preferred a more detailed review of various antibiotics, including interactions, which agents should be taken together with food, which ones should not be given to children, and so on. Neither in the index nor in the relevant chapter about antibiotics did I find mention of probiotics. Treatment with lactobacilli has repeatedly been shown to be safe and effective in children with acute infectious diarrhea (2,3), although I admit that the cited articles (2,3) were published after Current Diagnosis & Treatment in Infectious Diseases (1) (original studies included in the systematic review were not) (3). The second part of the book consists of chapters focusing on specific organ systems (i.e., central nervous system, the orbit, gastrointestinal tract, and so on), and the third section focuses on specific microbial agents. Of outstanding interest to a pediatric gastroenterologist should be life-threatening pediatric infections, which one may be forced to handle at any time. The chapter on infections of the central nervous system is good. The recommendations for eradication of carrier states with rifampicin (Haemophilus influenzae meningitis) in otherwise-treated patients may differ between countries. Also, the authors have not mentioned that one should gently shake the cerebrospinal fluid obtained from a lumbar puncture before one inspects the specimen to determine whether large amounts of inflammatory cells are present. Infections may follow gastrointestinal disease or treatment for gastrointestinal disease. Immunosuppressive treatment may lead to neutropenia. The chapters on neutropenia and sepsis syndrome are good. I especially enjoyed the schematic pictures in the chapter covering sepsis syndrome. The book contains general chapters about infectious diarrhea and hepatobiliary infections and also specific chapters related to a particular microbial agent (Rota virus, other gastrointestinal viruses, hepatitis, enterococci, enteritis caused by Escherichia coli, Shigella, Salmonella, Vibrio, Campylobacter, and Giardia). Additionally, nematodes and trematodes are reviewed. During my time in the Department of Infectious Disease, I regularly saw a patient with hepatitis C. Current Diagnosis & Treatment in Infectious Diseases was most useful in the investigation of her symptoms and signs. I also found the chapter about enteritis caused by E. coli, Shigella, and Salmonella clearly written and easily understood. WHAT IS GOOD ABOUT THIS BOOK There are more than 300 summary boxes in this book (e.g., on antibiotic use, basic principles, and so on). The boxes are often split into a pediatric section and an adult section. On the back cover text, the publisher also notes that the book is aimed at students, residents, general and family practitioners, pediatricians, and infectious disease specialists. Even for a pediatric gastroenterologist, a basic knowledge of infectious disease is important, for example, to rule out differential diagnoses to inflammatory bowel disease and celiac disease and also to manage bacterial and viral gastroenteritis. Hepatitis may also be caused by a number of microbial agents. The chapters in this book are well written, and the index is easily understood. WHAT COULD BE IMPROVED IN THE NEXT EDITION Some chapters (e.g., chapter 22 covering obstetric and gynecologic infections) come with extensive lists of references; however, in a number of chapters, the lack of detailed references (e.g., chapter 2 covering host defense) may not encourage the reader to pursue further study. In addition, the citation of general textbooks only is not of great value because the textbooks may not be available in every library. Alternatively, most medical professionals have access to articles from a large number of journals at low cost (hence, my preference for reference lists with journal references). Current Diagnosis & Treatment in Infectious Diseases has few illustrations and flowcharts. For the next edition, I would like to see more illustrations similar to those found in the chapter on sepsis syndrome and to that of the sequence of appearance of viral antigens and antibodies in acute hepatitis B virus. Additional illustrations would enhance the text greatly. For the next edition, I would also like to see color photographs of the most common “fever and rash” syndromes. Only reading about Varicella, enterovirus, Epstein-Barr virus, Parvovirus B19, etc. and studying gray-scale pictures will not make one an expert in rash diagnosis! Recently, Zoetendal et al. (5) showed that mucosa-associated bacteria in the human gastrointestinal tract differ from those recovered from the feces. Bacterial diversity in the gastrointestinal tract may be an underlying factor not only in gastrointestinal disease but also in other disease, e.g., atopic disease (6). The topic of microbial diversity in the large intestine is receiving increased attention. Therefore, it is surprising that this book provides limited coverage of the subject. In conclusion, the structure of this book is good. It is a good compromise between all-encompassing textbooks such as Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles & Practice of Infectious Diseases (4) and introductory texts. Those looking for an introductory text in infectious disease or an easily digestible book (i.e., lots of illustrations) should keep searching. For those with some knowledge of infectious disease looking for an accessible source of clinical information, Current Diagnosis & Treatment in Infectious Diseases may be the answer.
