TI  - Cultured Milk and Yogurt
AU  - Nagendra P. Shah
AU  - Claude P. Champagne
PY  - 2015
SP  - 235
EP  - 265
DO  - 10.1002/9781118810279.ch10
AB  - Cultured dairy products are the earliest form of food preservation. Recently, the perception of these products has changed throughout the world, particularly, with the introduction of different varieties such as fruit or sweetened yogurt and with probiotic organisms. There are many good reviews on the metabolic pathways linked to lactic fermentation by starter cultures. This chapter focuses on how technological parameters will affect the fermentation of milk by starters. These parameters will include milk composition, strain ratio in the culture, fermentation temperature, and redox level. Lactic acid bacteria (LAB) have many roles in milk fermentations. The chapter presents a summary of the effects of changes in starter composition, and It also addresses the factors, which affect the growth of the various species. It talks about composition of the milk matrix, inoculation practices, and cultured milks produced by mesophilic lactic starter cultures, thermophilic lactic starter cultures and mixed fermentation.
